[Opening "Benoni Defense: Classical Variation"]

{Benoni Defense: Classical Variation. Theory ends about here. From this point the game is decided by understanding rather than memory.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. e4 O-O {Both sides have what they wanted from the opening. The middlegame will be about the centre.} 6. Nf3 e6 7. Be2 exd5 8. cxd5 ! d6 {The point of the whole plan. Material is level, but only one side has a target to attack.} (8... c4 Rb1 Nxe4 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Nd2 Na6 10. O-O Nc7 11. a4 Rb8 ?! 12. f3 b6 13. Nb5 a6 14. Nxc7 Qxc7 15. Qc2 Nd7 16. Rb1 b5 17. b4 c4 18. a5 Ne5 19. Kh1 f5 20. Bb2 fxe4 21. Nxe4 Bf5 22. Rbd1 Rbe8 23. Bc3 Bxe4 24. fxe4 Qe7 25. Qd2 Qh4 26. Qd4 Rxf1+ 27. Rxf1 Rf8 28. Rxf8+ Bxf8 29. g3 Qf6 30. Qb6 h5 31. Kg2 h4 32. Qxa6 h3+ 33. Kxh3 Qf2 34. Bxe5 Qxe2 35. Bxd6 Qf1+ 36. Kg4 Qf6 37. Qxb5 Bxd6 38. Qxc4 Qe7 39. a6 Qd7+ 40. Kf3 Qf7+ 41. Kg2 Qe7 42. b5 Bc5 {The endgame is winning but not simple — the passed pawn needs its king, not just its rook.} 43. d6+ 1-0

