[Opening "Queen's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation, Kramnik Variation"]

{Queen's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation, Kramnik Variation. Both sides have what they wanted from the opening. The middlegame will be about the centre.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 b6 3. g3 e6 4. Bg2 Be7 5. c4 Bb7 {Theory ends about here. From this point the game is decided by understanding rather than memory.} 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 d5 8. cxd5 ! exd5 {The critical moment. The pieces are as good as they are going to get; something has to happen now.} (8... b5 Nbd2 b4 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Nc3 Nbd7 10. Bf4 c5 11. h4 Ne4 ?! 12. Rc1 Ndf6 13. e3 c4 14. a4 Re8 15. Qc2 Rc8 16. Ne5 Bd6 17. Nb5 Bb4 18. Red1 a6 19. Nc3 Bd6 20. Bg5 Qc7 21. Bxf6 Nxf6 22. f4 h5 23. Bf3 Qe7 24. Qg2 Qe6 25. Rd2 Rc7 26. Rf1 Rd8 27. Re2 Kh7 28. Kh1 Kh8 29. Kg1 Kh7 30. Ref2 {Resignation is timely: the king cannot be defended and the counterplay is one move too slow.} Kg8 1/2-1/2
